{"payload":{"candidate":{"prompt":"You are given JSON with two parts: 'system_profile' describing installed software (product names and versions) and 'advisories', a supplied list of CVE entries, each with an ID, description, and affected-product/version information. Your job is to decide which of the supplied advisories actually apply to the installed system, then return ONLY a JSON object of the form {\"applicable_cves\":[\"CVE-...\"]} containing unique advisory IDs taken verbatim from the supplied list (empty array if none apply). Do not output any other text.\n\nMatching rules:\n\n1. PRODUCT MATCHING. An advisory applies only if one of its affected products matches a product installed on the system. Product names may be written differently across the inventory and the advisories: underscores may appear instead of spaces (e.g. 'some_product' vs 'some product'), vendor prefixes/suffixes may be stripped or added (e.g. 'vendor product' vs 'product'), and package-manager style names may be used. Normalize names (case-insensitive, replace underscores with spaces, strip vendor prefixes, ignore punctuation) before comparing. Do not match a product merely because descriptions mention it in passing; the advisory must list it as affected.\n\n2. VERSION CHECKING — THIS IS THE CRITICAL STEP. Once a product matches, carefully compare the INSTALLED version against the advisory's affected version range. Parse versions as dot-separated numeric segments and compare segment by segment (pad with zeros so 1.2 compares correctly against 1.2.0 and 1.2.1). Respect every bound you are given:\n   - If the advisory states affected versions 'up to and including X' or 'X and below', the installed version applies if it is less than or equal to X.\n   - If it states 'from A' / 'A and later' / 'A to B', the installed version applies only if it is greater than or equal to A (and less than or equal to B when an upper bound exists).\n   - If the advisory says the issue is fixed in version F, or lists versions 'before F' as affected, then installed versions strictly below F apply and installed versions equal to or above F do NOT apply.\n   - Pay close attention to whether bounds are inclusive ('through', 'up to and including', '<=') or exclusive ('prior to', 'before', '<'). A version exactly equal to a stated bound is affected only when the bound is inclusive.\n   - Compare full version strings, not just the first one or two segments: 1.2.3 and 1.2.30 are different, and 1.9 may be lower than 1.10.\n\n3. AVOID THESE TWO ERROR PATTERNS:\n   - DO NOT exclude an advisory just because the installed version looks different in length or format from the bound (e.g. a 2-segment installed version vs a 3- or 4-segment bound). Normalize and compare numerically; if the installed version falls inside the affected range, the advisory APPLIES.\n   - DO NOT include an advisory when the installed version is outside the affected range (e.g. at or above a fixed version, or below a stated 'affected from' lower bound). Being the right product is not enough; the version must satisfy the advisory's bounds.\n\n4. If an advisory gives no version constraints for a matched product, treat it as applying to the installed product.\n5. If version information is ambiguous or cannot be parsed, include the advisory only if the product matches and nothing in the advisory clearly rules out the installed version.\n6. Each applicable ID must appear at most once; deduplicate. Use IDs exactly as supplied.\n7. Consider every advisory in the supplied list independently; do not stop early or skip entries.\n\nReturn only the JSON object: {\"applicable_cves\":[\"CVE-...\"]}"},"handle":"13aa3fa7d12af1d93802b015ceeb216a35dfded3ba2f042c4a9eef743bffb925","profile_ids":["optimization_0135","optimization_0144","optimization_0133","optimization_0058","optimization_0077","optimization_0140","optimization_0231","optimization_0030","optimization_0073","optimization_0232","optimization_0065","optimization_0236","o